<div class="stonetop-foundry-basics">

  <div class="stonetop-foundry-basics-scroll">

    <header class="stonetop-fb-hero">
      <h2>New to <span class="stonetop-fb-logo">Foundry</span>?</h2>
      <p class="stonetop-fb-lead">
        A two-minute tour of the handful of things that trip up first-time GMs. Nothing here is Stonetop-specific magic &mdash; it's just how Foundry keeps your stuff organised. Once these click, the rest is easy.
      </p>
    </header>

    <ul class="stonetop-fb-cards">

      <li class="stonetop-fb-card">
        <i class="fas fa-layer-group stonetop-fb-card-icon"></i>
        <div class="stonetop-fb-card-body">
          <h3>Two places things live</h3>
          <p>Everything in Foundry sits in one of two spots:</p>
          <ul class="stonetop-fb-list">
            <li><strong>Your world</strong> &mdash; the game you're running. The sidebar on the right (Journals, Actors, Items&hellip;) is all yours to add to, edit, and delete.</li>
            <li><strong>Compendiums</strong> &mdash; read-only libraries of pre-made content, like a sealed reference book. Stonetop ships its journals, monsters, and items as compendiums. You'll find them under the <strong>book</strong> tab (<i class="fas fa-atlas"></i> Compendium Packs) in the sidebar.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stonetop-fb-card">
        <i class="fas fa-lock stonetop-fb-card-icon"></i>
        <div class="stonetop-fb-card-body">
          <h3>Compendiums can't be edited</h3>
          <p>That's on purpose &mdash; a compendium is the pristine original. To change anything from one, you first make a copy of it <em>in your world</em> (this is called <strong>importing</strong>), then edit the copy. The original always stays put, so you can never break it.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stonetop-fb-card">
        <i class="fas fa-book-open stonetop-fb-card-icon"></i>
        <div class="stonetop-fb-card-body">
          <h3>Your journals are already imported</h3>
          <p>Good news: you don't have to import the journals. The first time you opened this world, Stonetop copied <strong>every</strong> journal &mdash; the Setting Overview, locations, lore, and the bestiary &mdash; straight into your <strong>Journals</strong> sidebar.</p>
          <p>So open the <strong>Journals</strong> tab, not the compendium, and edit those entries freely. They're your copies; change anything you like.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stonetop-fb-card">
        <i class="fas fa-clock-rotate-left stonetop-fb-card-icon"></i>
        <div class="stonetop-fb-card-body">
          <h3>So when <em>would</em> you open the journal compendium?</h3>
          <p>Almost never. The only real reason is to peek at the <strong>original wording</strong> of an entry after you've edited your own copy &mdash; a "what did this say before I changed it?" reference. Day to day, you can ignore the <strong>Stonetop</strong> journal compendium entirely.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stonetop-fb-card">
        <i class="fas fa-dragon stonetop-fb-card-icon"></i>
        <div class="stonetop-fb-card-body">
          <h3>Monsters: import the one you want to use</h3>
          <p>Monsters are the exception &mdash; they're <em>not</em> pre-loaded into your world (that would clutter it with 100+ creatures you may never use). When you want to throw a monster at the players, open the <strong>Monsters</strong> compendium, find it, and <strong>drag it onto the canvas</strong> or right-click it and choose <strong>Import</strong>.</p>
          <p>That puts a copy in your <strong>Actors</strong> sidebar, ready to drop onto the map.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

      <li class="stonetop-fb-card">
        <i class="fas fa-heart-pulse stonetop-fb-card-icon"></i>
        <div class="stonetop-fb-card-body">
          <h3>Each imported monster is its own copy</h3>
          <p>Once a monster is in your world it's a standalone actor. Track its <strong>HP</strong>, tweak its moves, rename it &mdash; those changes only touch <em>that</em> copy. Import three wolves and you've got three separate wolves to wound and track independently, and the compendium original never changes. Bruised it last fight? Re-import a fresh one any time.</p>
        </div>
      </li>

    </ul>

    <div class="stonetop-fb-tips">
      <h3><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> A few habits that help</h3>
      <ul class="stonetop-fb-list">
        <li><strong>Right-click</strong> almost anything &mdash; a sidebar entry, a token, a compendium item &mdash; for a menu of what you can do with it (Import, Duplicate, Delete&hellip;).</li>
        <li><strong>Drag and drop</strong> is everywhere: pull a monster onto the map, drag an item onto a character sheet.</li>
        <li>Deleting a copy in your world is safe &mdash; the compendium original is never affected, so you can always import it again (though the fresh copy won't carry any changes you'd made).</li>
        <li>Stuck? The <strong>search</strong> box at the top of each sidebar tab finds things fast.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="stonetop-fb-outro">
      <p>That's the whole trick: <strong>your world is yours to edit; compendiums are read-only originals you copy from.</strong> Journals are already copied in for you; monsters you copy in as you need them.</p>
    </div>

  </div>

</div>
